<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Deng</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 BCE – 678 BCE (approx.) · capital Deng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small kingdom of the Man clan on the Han River, older than the Zhou, whose marriage ties with Chu did not save it from being swallowed by that rising power in 678 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Deng appears in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang</a> oracle-bone inscriptions and survived the Zhou conquest as a minor state ruled by the Man clan, centred near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyang-henan">Nanyang</a> basin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. It lay on the route between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-plain-china">Central Plain</a> and the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded of its institutions. Deng is known mainly through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo zhuan</a>, which notes marriages between its ruling house and the kings of Chu: Deng Man was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-chu">King Wu of Chu</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-chu">King Wen</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> vessels inscribed with the name of Deng rulers have been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-chu">King Wen of Chu</a> passed through Deng on campaign, the Marquis of Deng refused his advisers' urging to kill his visiting nephew. Chu attacked Deng soon afterwards and annexed it in 678 BCE, an early step in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a>'s expansion northwards.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-deng.jpg" alt="State of Deng" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 BCE – 678 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Deng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Deng</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-939&amp;focus=state-of-deng" aria-label="State of Deng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7427 971 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Deng lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7909 1324h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7747 1227h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7747 1227h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-939&amp;focus=state-of-deng">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

